WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 253: Thomas Edison: The Wizard of Menlo Park, the Incandescent Lamp, and the Power Grid

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.760
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the spark of human genius that illuminated the modern industrial world.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.840
[Rebecca] The man who banished nighttime darkness and captured the human voice.

5
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:26.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the unyielding life of the Wizard of Menlo Park, Thomas Alva Edison.

6
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:45.840
[George] Born in February 1847 in the Canal Boomtown of Milan, Ohio, Thomas Alva Edison was an intensely inquisitive, restless child whose formal schooling lasted barely 12 weeks, when an exasperated schoolmaster declared young Al to be addled and unteachable.

7
00:00:45.840 --> 00:01:02.720
[George] His fiercely protective mother Nancy Elliott Edison, a former Canadian schoolteacher, withdrew him immediately, transforming their home into a private academy where she nurtured his insatiable curiosity through classics of chemistry, Newtonian physics, and natural philosophy.

8
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:19.520
[George] By age 12, Edison was hawking newspapers and candy on the Grand Trunk Railroad between Port Huron and Detroit, setting up a secret chemical laboratory and a hand-cranked printing press in an empty baggage car to publish his own weekly newspaper, the Grand Trunk Herald.

9
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:38.960
[George] During his teenage years, a bout of scarlet fever combined with a train conductor pulling him aboard by his ears left Edison severely hearing impaired, a condition he later credited as a profound blessing that spared him the distracting chatter of the world and allowed him to concentrate with laser intensity on mechanical problems.

10
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:48.720
[George] After saving the three-year-old son of a station agent from being crushed by a runaway boxcar, the grateful father taught Edison the art of railway telegraphy.

11
00:01:48.720 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] Working as an itinerant tramp telegrapher across the Midwest during the Civil War, Edison spent nights dissecting batteries, Morse keys, and electrical circuits, mastering the invisible currents of electricity.

12
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:13.640
[George] Edison's mother introduced him to Richard Green Parker's School of Natural Philosophy, conducting every chemical experiment described in the textbook until an explosion blew out the basement windows.

13
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:24.840
[George] His hearing loss proved to be a psychological shield, insulating him from ambient noise and allowing him to sleep soundly amid the thunderous vibrations of heavy industrial machinery.

14
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:41.160
[George] Arriving penniless in New York City in 1869, Edison's fortunes changed overnight when he repaired a broken stock ticker in the Gold Indicator Company's trading room during a financial panic, winning a lucrative contract to engineer an improved universal stock ticker.

15
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:52.120
[George] When Wall Street financiers paid him an astronomical $40,000 for his patent, 22-year-old Edison poured the fortune into machine shops in Newark, New Jersey.

16
00:02:52.120 --> 00:03:05.400
[George] Developing the revolutionary quadruplex telegraph, a system capable of sending two separate messages in each direction simultaneously over a single copper wire, saving millions of dollars for Western Union.

17
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:50.400
[George] In 1876, Edison purchased a rural tract of land along the Pennsylvania Railroad in Menlo Park, New Jersey, constructing the world's first dedicated industrial research and development laboratory, a two story timber building packed with thousands of chemical reagents, precision lathes, galvanometers and musical instruments, gathering an elite multidisciplinary team of European machinists, glassblowers, mathematicians, and chemists, nicknamed the Insomnia Squad, Edison transformed invention from the solitary hobby of eccentric loaners into an organized, collective industrial science, audaciously promising to deliver a minor invention every ten days and a big thing every six months.

18
00:03:50.400 --> 00:04:02.480
[George] The quadruplex telegraph revolutionized transatlantic news reporting, allowing newspapers like the New York Herald to receive continuous market quotes and international dispatches on a single circuit.

19
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:15.280
[George] Menlo Park became a scientific boomtown, illuminated by gas jets and buzzing with the whirr of steam-driven lathes, attracting curious tourists and foreign dignitaries to the sleepy New Jersey countryside.

20
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:32.880
[George] In the autumn of 1877, while working to improve Alexander Graham Bell's telephone transmitter using carbonized button microphones, Edison noticed that a vibrating diaphragm attached to a telephone receiver made a prickling indentation on a paper tape coated with paraffin wax.

21
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:45.360
[George] Struck by an electrifying flash of insight, Edison realized that if sound vibrations could produce physical indentations, those same mechanical indentations could be retraced to recreate the human voice.

22
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:58.080
[George] Sketching a simple diagram of a brass cylinder grooved with a spiral thread and wrapped in tin foil, Edison handed the drawing to his master Swiss machinist John Crusy with the simple instruction Make this.

23
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.320
[George] Cruzi bet Edison a box of cigars that the device would never work.

24
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:09.040
[George] When the machine was assembled, Edison turned the hand crank and spoke into the metal diaphragm the nursery rhyme.

25
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:12.640
[George] Mary had a little lamb, its fleece was white as snow.

26
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.800
[George] Reversing the cylinder and resting the stylus against the indented foil, Edison turned the crank again.

27
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:27.200
[George] To the absolute astonishment of the exhausted mechanics, the little machine spoke back Edison's exact words in a faint, metallic whisper.

28
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.320
[George] Edison was completely awestruck, later confessing, I was never so taken aback in my life.

29
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.000
[George] Everybody was astonished.

30
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.520
[George] I was always afraid of things that worked the first time.

31
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:48.360
[George] Overnight, world newspapers hailed the 30-year-old inventor as the wizard of Menlo Park, crowds swarmed his laboratory, and President Rutherford B.

32
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.880
[George] Hayes summoned Edison to the White House to demonstrate the talking machine past midnight.

33
00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:06.600
[George] Edison's phonograph captured the imagination of the Victorian world, inspiring early science fiction authors and prompting the French Academy of Sciences to investigate whether a ventriloquist was hidden beneath the table.

34
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:17.720
[George] When the cylinder was played before President Hayes in the White House, First Lady Lucy Hayes wept with emotion upon hearing her own laughter recorded and played back with eerie acoustic clarity.

35
00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:26.120
[George] In 1878, Edison turned his sights toward the greatest technological challenge of the 19th century, the electric light.

36
00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:37.640
[George] Arc lamps were already used to light city streets, but they were blindingly bright, hissed violently, emitted toxic fumes, and were impossible to subdivide for domestic living rooms.

37
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:45.960
[George] Edison envisioned a small, soft, incandescent bulb that could safely replace dangerous gas lamps in millions of private homes.

38
00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.400
[George] The scientific world declared the idea impossible.

39
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:57.320
[George] Leading British physicists published papers proving that subdividing the electric current would violate fundamental thermodynamic laws.

40
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:03.240
[George] Undaunted, Edison and his team launched an epic campaign of systematic trial and error.

41
00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:17.520
[George] Testing over 6,000 organic and metallic materials for an incandescent filament, including platinum, tungsten, cedar, boxwood, coconut hair, fishline, and even hairs plucked from the beard of his laboratory assistants.

42
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:20.880
[George] The breakthrough arrived in late October 1879.

43
00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:33.840
[George] Using a high vacuum mercury pump to evacuate a glass globe and baking an ordinary piece of cotton sewing thread into pure carbon, Edison sealed the fragile horseshoe filament inside the bulb and applied current.

44
00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:42.560
[George] The bulb began to glow with a beautiful, steady, mellow yellow light, burning continuously for over 13 hours before expiring.

45
00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:58.400
[George] On New Year's Eve 1879, Edison wired Menlo Park with hundreds of incandescent lamps, welcoming over 3,000 stunned visitors who rode special midnight trains from New York to gaze upon a fairy tale spectacle of pure electric illumination.

46
00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:11.600
[George] Edison sent botanical expeditions to the jungles of the Amazon, the highlands of Japan, and the swamps of Florida, testing bamboo fibers that burned for over 1,200 hours in evacuated glass globes.

47
00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:24.400
[George] The New Year's Eve demonstration at Menlo Park overwhelmed the Pennsylvania Railroad, which had to run extra passenger trains through the night to accommodate thousands of spectators marveling at the glowing glass orbs.

48
00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.920
[George] Edison understood what other inventors failed to grasp.

49
00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:34.320
[George] An electric light bulb was utterly useless without an entire municipal electrical infrastructure to power it.

50
00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:42.160
[George] Between 1880 and 1882, Edison engineered every single component of a commercial electrical grid from scratch.

51
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:54.560
[George] Designing high-efficiency bipolar dynamos nicknamed jumbos, subterranean insulated copper cables, safety fuses, threaded screw-in-lamp sockets, meters, and wall switches.

52
00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:05.160
[George] Digging trenches through the cobblestone streets of Lower Manhattan, Edison constructed the pearl Street central generating station, housing six giant steam-driven dynamos.

53
00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:15.640
[George] On the afternoon of September 4, 1882, 35-year-old Thomas Edison, wearing a white derby hat, stood in the offices of financier J.P.

54
00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.360
[George] Morgan on Wall Street and gave the signal.

55
00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.240
[George] The chief engineer threw the master switch.

56
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:32.440
[George] Instantly, 400 incandescent lamps flared into brilliant, silent light across 59 customer buildings, including the New York Times newsroom and Morgan's banking halls.

57
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.720
[George] It was the birth of the modern commercial power grid.

58
00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:47.560
[George] For the first time in human history, electricity was delivered as a clean, centralized, metered public utility, banishing the centuries-old tyranny of fire and darkness from urban life.

59
00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:59.400
[George] Pearl Street's subterranean conduit tubes were insulated with a secret tar and beeswax mixture, laid by Italian laborers working by torchlight under the watchful supervision of Edison himself.

60
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:13.080
[George] When the Wall Street switches were thrown, stockbrokers and bankers cheered as incandescent lamps illuminated trading desks, proving that electricity was cleaner, safer, and brighter than dangerous open gas flames.

61
00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:20.280
[George] Edison's Pearl Street system relied on direct current, DC, which flowed in one direction at low voltage.

62
00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:25.080
[George] While safe and reliable, DC possessed a fatal physical limitation.

63
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:35.720
[George] It suffered massive resistance losses and could only transmit power within a radius of one to two miles, requiring a noisy generating station on nearly every city corner.

64
00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:54.560
[George] In the late 1880s, industrialist George Westinghouse and Serbian American genius Nikola Tesla introduced alternating current AC, which could be stepped up to thousands of volts by transformers, transmitted over hundreds of miles over thin wires, and stepped down safely at the consumer's home.

65
00:10:54.560 --> 00:11:09.200
[George] Fearing that his multimillion dollar DC infrastructure would be made obsolete, Edison waged an aggressive, acrimonious public campaign against AC, warning that high voltage currents were lethal and unsafe for residential neighborhoods.

66
00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:23.200
[George] Edison's associates staged sensational public demonstrations electrocuting stray animals with AC generators and backed the adoption of an AC generator for the world's first electric chair at Auburn Prison in 1890.

67
00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.360
[George] Sarcastically dubbing the execution method getting Westinghoused.

68
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:34.800
[George] Yet despite Edison's fierce resistance, the superior physics and economic efficiency of alternating current triumphed.

69
00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:48.080
[George] Westinghouse and Tesla won the contract to illuminate the 1893 Chicago World's Fair and harness the hydroelectric power of Niagara Falls, permanently establishing AC as the global transmission standard.

70
00:11:48.080 --> 00:12:00.560
[George] Edison's stubborn resistance to alternating current was rooted in his personal experience with low-voltage direct current systems, convincing him that high voltage transformers were an unnecessary public hazard.

71
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:12.240
[George] The adoption of AC at Niagara Falls marked the end of the war of the currents, though Edison later graciously admitted that alternating current was the greatest electrical achievement of the 19th century.

72
00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:20.480
[George] Moving to a sprawling, state of the art laboratory complex in West Orange, New Jersey, Edison refused to slow down.

73
00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:28.320
[George] Collaborating with Scottish engineer William Kennedy Dixon, Edison invented the kinetoscope and kindograph in 1891.

74
00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:40.280
[George] Using George Eastman's flexible celluloid film to capture moving images and establishing the world's first motion picture studio, a revolving black tar paper building nicknamed the Black Maria.

75
00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:52.520
[George] Edison later perfected alkaline nickel iron storage batteries for electric vehicles and submarines, operated massive magnetic iron or separating mills, and manufactured phonograph records.

76
00:12:52.520 --> 00:13:08.440
[George] Across his 60-year career, Edison embodied a work ethic of legendary superhuman tenacity, sleeping for four hours a night on laboratory workbenches and famously defining genius as 1% inspiration and 99% perspiration.

77
00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:19.800
[George] When Thomas Edison died in October 1931 at age 84, President Herbert Hoover requested that all Americans dim their electric lights for one minute in nationwide tribute.

78
00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:31.880
[George] As the lights across the continent faded into darkness, the American people realized how entirely their world had been illuminated by the tireless hands and boundless imagination of one deaf Ohio boy.

79
00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:44.040
[George] The West Orange Laboratory encompassed over 20 buildings, including a massive library with a three-story fireplace, precision machine shops, and chemical stockrooms housing every known element.

80
00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:58.680
[George] Edison's legacy transformed the United States into an industrial superpower, proving that systematic research, boundless curiosity, and unyielding determination could reshape the material reality of human civilization.

81
00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:02.440
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

82
00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:09.240
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 254.

83
00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:15.920
[Rebecca] Nicola Tesla, the Master of Lightning, The AC Induction Motor, Niagara Falls, and Wardencliffe.

84
00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.080
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

